In the semiconductor industry, "ASIC design" is split into two halves:

Front-End: Writing RTL, synthesizing it into logic gates (Yosys), and checking timing/power (OpenSTA).
Back-End (Physical Design): Floorplanning, placing the gates on a physical grid, routing copper wires, and generating the GDSII file (OpenROAD).
By sticking to Yosys and OpenSTA, we are doing the entire Front-End ASIC flow, which is perfectly sufficient for an RTL optimization hackathon. We are skipping the Back-End flow because it takes hours of compute time and isn't strictly required to prove our AI works.

